assistantMessageEvent.content:
The race: in `flights.run`, when a flight is detached via `forget(key)` (e.g., invalidate), a new `get` starts a new flight. But when the old flight resolves, it calls `pending.delete(key)` unconditionally — that would delete the *newer* flight's entry from the pending map, and `commit(value)` would store the stale value over a newer value or install a value that was invalidated.

Two bugs:
1. Old flight's `pending.delete(key)` removes the newer flight from the registry → deduplication breaks; subsequent gets would start yet another load instead of joining the newer flight.
2. Old flight's `commit(value)` writes the stale value into `values`, overwriting values installed by `invalidate`/`set` or newer flights, and restarting TTL. Contract: "An obsolete flight... cannot store a value or detach a newer flight."

Fix: capture the promise/record and on settle, only delete from pending if the pending entry is still this flight; only commit if the flight is still the registered one... Wait — but if the flight was detached (invalidated), commit must NOT store. If the flight is still registered (normal case), commit stores. So commit should only happen if `pending.get(key) === promise` at settle time? But note: what if value expired... no, values map is separate. Contract says: obsolete flight cannot store a value. So commit only if pending.get(key) === this promise. But wait — `set()` also detaches flight and installs value; if old flight commits later, must not overwrite. If flight still registered, commit proceeds.

Also self-read case: "A load may also read its own key without awaiting it; the latter joins its existing flight." That works via pending.has.

Also reentrancy: "load may call invalidate, set, or clear synchronously before returning; the same barrier rules apply." In `run`, `work` is invoked in `Promise.resolve().then(work)` — so load is invoked asynchronously, after `pending.set`. If load calls invalidate synchronously during work() execution, pending has been set already, so invalidate's forget removes it; then this flight's settle must not commit. With the check `pending.get(key) === promise` at settle time, commit skipped. Good.

But there's a subtlety: the promise variable is referenced inside the then callback before assignment. `const promise = Promise.resolve().then(work).then(...)` — the callback referencing `promise` runs later, so fine.

