The race: in `flights.run`, when a flight is detached via `forget(key)` (e.g., invalidate), a new `get` starts a new flight. But when the old flight resolves, it calls `pending.delete(key)` unconditionally — that would delete the *newer* flight's entry from the pending map, and `commit(value)` would store the stale value over a newer value or install a value that was invalidated.

Two bugs:
1. Old flight's `pending.delete(key)` removes the newer flight from the registry → deduplication breaks; subsequent gets would start yet another load instead of joining the newer flight.
2. Old flight's `commit(value)` writes the stale value into `values`, overwriting values installed by `invalidate`/`set` or newer flights, and restarting TTL. Contract: "An obsolete flight... cannot store a value or detach a newer flight."

Fix: capture the promise/record and on settle, only delete from pending if the pending entry is still this flight; only commit if the flight is still the registered one... Wait — but if the flight was detached (invalidated), commit must NOT store. If the flight is still registered (normal case), commit stores. So commit should only happen if `pending.get(key) === promise` at settle time? But note: what if value expired... no, values map is separate. Contract says: obsolete flight cannot store a value. So commit only if pending.get(key) === this promise. But wait — `set()` also detaches flight and installs value; if old flight commits later, must not overwrite. If flight still registered, commit proceeds.

Also self-read case: "A load may also read its own key without awaiting it; the latter joins its existing flight." That works via pending.has.

Also reentrancy: "load may call invalidate, set, or clear synchronously before returning; the same barrier rules apply." In `run`, `work` is invoked in `Promise.resolve().then(work)` — so load is invoked asynchronously, after `pending.set`. If load calls invalidate synchronously during work() execution, pending has been set already, so invalidate's forget removes it; then this flight's settle must not commit. With the check `pending.get(key) === promise` at settle time, commit skipped. Good.

But there's a subtlety: the promise variable is referenced inside the then callback before assignment. `const promise = Promise.resolve().then(work).then(...)` — the callback referencing `promise` runs later, so fine.

Also need to handle rejection: currently rejection also goes through `pending.delete(key)` — wait, no: `.then(work).then(value => {...})` — on rejection of work, the second `.then`'s onFulfilled isn't called, so `pending.delete(key)` never runs, and the rejection propagates, leaving the rejected promise stuck in `pending` forever. Subsequent gets would return the rejected promise instead of retrying! Contract: "Rejected loads... are never cached... a subsequent get may retry." That's the "related failure path" bug. So on rejection, we must remove from pending (only if still ours) and rethrow.

Fix in flight.js:

```js
run(key, work, commit) {
  if (pending.has(key)) return pending.get(key);
  const promise = Promise.resolve().then(work).then(
    value => {
      if (pending.get(key) === promise) {
        pending.delete(key);
        commit(value);
      }
      return value;
    },
    reason => {
      if (pending.get(key) === promise) pending.delete(key);
      throw reason;
    }
  );
  pending.set(key, promise);
  return promise;
}
```

Wait — but commit timing: TTL starts when flight commits. Contract: "A successful value's TTL starts when that flight commits". Commit happens in the then callback — fine.

Edge: what if a flight settles while registered, commit stores value. Then later invalidate. Fine.

Edge: flight settles, is still registered, commits; but commit... in cache.js, commit sets values. But what if between settle and... it's synchronous within the then. Fine.

Edge: What if flight detached and a new flight registered, then old flight settles: `pending.get(key) === promise` false → no delete, no commit. Original waiters still get the value. ✓

Edge: rejection while detached: same, no delete (protects newer flight), reason propagates to original waiters. ✓

Edge: rejection while registered: delete and rethrow; subsequent get retries. But the same rejected promise object is stored in pending briefly — all current waiters get the same rejection reason. ✓ But careful: a waiter who joins after work rejects but before the rejection handler runs? Microtask ordering: work rejects → the second then's onRejected is queued. In between, could a get run? Only from another microtask/task. If the onRejected handler hasn't run yet, pending still has the rejected promise and get would join it, receiving same rejection. Contract says all waiters receive the same rejection reason — acceptable ("a subsequent get may retry", may). Hmm, but is that fine? "Rejected loads are never cached: all waiters receive the same rejection reason, and a subsequent get may retry." Joining an already-settled-but-not-yet-cleaned flight — the waiter gets the same rejection. That seems contract-conformant.

Another subtle thing: unhandled rejection warnings? If a waiter joins, fine.

Also cache.js `get`: expired value deletion, then flights.run starts new load. "If it expires while an old load is pending, get starts a new load, not the detached one" — about set(). In cache.set, flights.forget then values.set. Fine with fix.

One more check: `clock.expired` uses `now() >= deadline`, deadline = insertion + ttl. With ttlMs=0: settle at time T, deadline = T, immediately expired → never reuses settled value, but dedupes unsettled flight. ✓ — commit stores value but it's expired immediately. Fine.

Also `size` discards expired without detaching flights. ✓ Already.

